resume_schema_hint:
Respond with ONLY a JSON object matching this exact shape, no other text:
{
  "fullName": "the candidate's full name, exactly as it appears at the top of the resume",
  "email": "the candidate's email address, if present",
  "phone": "the candidate's phone number, if present",
  "summary": "a short 1-3 sentence professional summary/headline capturing who this person is professionally",
  "experience": [
    {"title": "job title", "company": "company name", "startDate": "e.g. 2021 or Jan 2021", "endDate": "e.g. Present or 2023", "description": "the specific responsibilities/achievements in this role, as written in the resume — do not compress this down to a generic one-liner if the resume gives more detail than that; keep specific tools, methodologies, and types of work mentioned (e.g. what kind of testing, what kind of development) rather than flattening them into a vague summary"}
  ],
  "education": [
    {"school": "institution name", "degree": "e.g. Bachelor of Science", "field": "e.g. Computer Science", "startDate": "", "endDate": ""}
  ],
  "projects": [
    {"name": "project name", "description": "1-2 sentences on what it is/does", "technologies": "comma-separated technologies used, if mentioned"}
  ],
  "skills": ["skill1", "skill2", "..."],
  "languages": ["e.g. Spanish (fluent), German (conversational) — include proficiency only if the resume states it"],
  "certifications": ["certification name, with issuer and/or year if the resume gives them"],
  "interests": ["interest or hobby 1", "..."]
}
Extract only what's actually present in the resume text — do not invent experience, dates, skills, languages,
certifications, or contact details that aren't there. Use "" for a missing fullName/email/phone and an empty
array for any section with nothing to extract. Keep descriptions concise, but do not drop specific, concrete
details (a named tool, a specific type of work, a certification's issuer) just to shorten something.

parse_resume_template:
You are extracting structured information from a resume/CV so it can be reused later. Read the
resume text below and pull out the candidate's professional summary, work experience, education, projects,
skills, and interests/hobbies.

RESUME TEXT:
{resume_text}

{resume_schema_hint}

cover_letter.notes_block_template:
The candidate specifically wants these points included (their own words, weave them in naturally):
{user_notes}
Only use these notes for whatever specific thing they actually say — if they name one technology or fact, don't repurpose that sentence for a different requirement from the job description below.

answer.template:
{anti_fabrication_rule}

{skill_whitelist_block}

You are helping a candidate prepare for a job interview by drafting an answer to one specific question, in
their own voice — first person, conversational, like something a person would actually say out loud in an
interview, not a formal written document. Ground the answer in real, specific details from their background
below where relevant (name a real project, a real skill, a real piece of experience) rather than speaking in
generalities — but only real details that are actually written below. Keep it natural — contractions are
fine, it doesn't need to be polished prose. Around 100-200 words, unless the question genuinely calls for
more. Plain text only, no markdown.

CANDIDATE PROFILE:
{profile}

JOB THEY'RE INTERVIEWING FOR:
{application}
{empty_profile_note}
INTERVIEW QUESTION: {question}

Write only the answer, nothing else. Reminder: {anti_fabrication_rule}
